{"protocol":"mindblown-brain/1","brain":"programming","page":{"id":"dsa/sorting","title":"Sorting","type":"concept","lobe":"dsa","desc":"Putting things in order; comparison sorts cannot beat Ω(n log n), and the interesting differences are stability, memory and behaviour on real data.","intro":"Putting things in order; comparison sorts cannot beat Ω(n log n), and the interesting differences are stability, memory and behaviour on real data.","created":"2026-09-01","updated":"2026-11-02","synapses":[{"id":"dsa/sorting#1","page":"dsa/sorting","lead":"The n log n floor","text":"Any comparison sort needs Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst case; counting and radix sorts sidestep it by not comparing.","section":"Synapses","links":["dsa/big-o"],"cites":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_sort"]},{"id":"dsa/sorting#2","page":"dsa/sorting","lead":"Stability","text":"A stable sort keeps equal elements in their original order, which matters when sorting by one key after another.","section":"Synapses","links":["dsa/sorting/mergesort"],"cites":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_algorithm"]},{"id":"dsa/sorting#3","page":"dsa/sorting","lead":"What the libraries actually use","text":"Python and Java (objects) use Timsort; C++ std::sort is introsort; Rust's stable sort is a merge variant and unstable sort a pattern-defeating quicksort.","section":"Synapses","links":["dsa/sorting/timsort","dsa/sorting/quicksort"],"cites":["https://docs.python.org/3/","https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/"]}],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_sort","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_algorithm","https://docs.python.org/3/","https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/"],"sections":["Synapses"]},"url":"https://mindblown.pplx.app/programming.html#n=dsa%2Fsorting","parent":null,"children":[{"id":"dsa/sorting/quicksort","title":"Quicksort","type":"concept","lobe":"dsa","parent":"dsa/sorting","desc":"Tony Hoare's 1959 divide-and-conquer sort: pick a pivot, partition, recurse. O(n log n) on average, O(n²) worst case, in place and cache-friendly.","created":"2026-09-01","updated":"2026-11-02","synapses":2,"sources":3,"children":0,"links":3,"url":"https://mindblown.pplx.app/programming.html#n=dsa%2Fsorting%2Fquicksort","api":"api/programming/v1/pages/dsa~2fsorting~2fquicksort.json"},{"id":"dsa/sorting/mergesort","title":"Mergesort","type":"concept","lobe":"dsa","parent":"dsa/sorting","desc":"Split, sort each half, merge; described by John von Neumann in 1945. Guaranteed O(n log n), stable, needs O(n) extra space.","created":"2026-09-01","updated":"2026-11-02","synapses":2,"sources":1,"children":0,"links":2,"url":"https://mindblown.pplx.app/programming.html#n=dsa%2Fsorting%2Fmergesort","api":"api/programming/v1/pages/dsa~2fsorting~2fmergesort.json"},{"id":"dsa/sorting/timsort","title":"Timsort","type":"concept","lobe":"dsa","parent":"dsa/sorting","desc":"Tim Peters' hybrid of merge sort and insertion sort (2002), built for Python; it finds runs that are already ordered and merges them, so nearly sorted data sorts in near-linear time.","created":"2026-09-01","updated":"2026-11-02","synapses":2,"sources":2,"children":0,"links":4,"url":"https://mindblown.pplx.app/programming.html#n=dsa%2Fsorting%2Ftimsort","api":"api/programming/v1/pages/dsa~2fsorting~2ftimsort.json"}],"synapses":[{"id":"dsa/sorting#1","page":"dsa/sorting","lead":"The n log n floor","text":"Any comparison sort needs Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst case; counting and radix sorts sidestep it by not comparing.","section":"Synapses","links":["dsa/big-o"],"cites":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_sort"]},{"id":"dsa/sorting#2","page":"dsa/sorting","lead":"Stability","text":"A stable sort keeps equal elements in their original order, which matters when sorting by one key after another.","section":"Synapses","links":["dsa/sorting/mergesort"],"cites":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_algorithm"]},{"id":"dsa/sorting#3","page":"dsa/sorting","lead":"What the libraries actually use","text":"Python and Java (objects) use Timsort; C++ std::sort is introsort; Rust's stable sort is a merge variant and unstable sort a pattern-defeating quicksort.","section":"Synapses","links":["dsa/sorting/timsort","dsa/sorting/quicksort"],"cites":["https://docs.python.org/3/","https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/"]}],"sources":[{"id":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_sort","label":"Wikipedia · Comparison sort","kind":"web","pages":["dsa/sorting"]},{"id":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_algorithm","label":"Wikipedia · Sorting algorithm","kind":"web","pages":["dsa/sorting"]},{"id":"https://docs.python.org/3/","label":"Python 3 documentation","kind":"web","pages":["lang/python","lang/python/cpython-gil","para/concurrency/async-await","dsa/hash-tables","dsa/sorting","dsa/sorting/timsort","sys/memory/refcount","sys/floating-point","craft/documentation"]},{"id":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/","label":"The Rust Programming Language (book)","kind":"web","pages":["lang/rust","lang/rust/ownership","lang/rust/cargo","para/type-systems/generics","para/type-systems/adts","para/type-systems/inference","para/concurrency/data-races","dsa/hash-tables","dsa/trees/bst","dsa/sorting","sys/memory/refcount","craft/package-managers","craft/documentation"]}],"neighbours":{"out":[{"page":"programming","w":1,"kinds":["mention"],"notes":["Algorithms outlive languages"]},{"page":"dsa/sorting/quicksort","w":2,"kinds":["see-also","mention"],"notes":["What the libraries actually use"]},{"page":"dsa/sorting/mergesort","w":2,"kinds":["see-also","mention"],"notes":["Stability"]},{"page":"dsa/sorting/timsort","w":2,"kinds":["see-also","mention"],"notes":["What the libraries actually use"]},{"page":"hist/knuth","w":1,"kinds":["mention"],"notes":["TAOCP"]}],"in":[{"page":"dsa/big-o","w":2,"kinds":["see-also","mention"],"notes":["The n log n floor"]}],"synapsesIn":[{"synapse":"programming#3","page":"programming"},{"synapse":"hist/knuth#1","page":"hist/knuth"}],"cocite":[{"page":"lang/python","w